    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "754f32f9_c596fac7",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/uicc/IccCardProxy.java",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 399,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-05-16T18:28:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, Applications can\u0027t actually use card-Apps until the state is READY, so for actually using a card-App, UNKNOWN vs NOT_READY is necessarily a don\u0027t-care. But setting this to NOT_READY allows us to differentiate between when the card is present and when the card is not present, and the state is definitely not \"UNKNOWN\": if we reach here, we are confident that the card is present.\n\n|  That sounds ok to me, but are we sure this change in semantics won\u0027t break any apps\n\nThat would be very hard to prove definitively, but given my assertion above (that for trying to use an App, anything other than READY is more-or-less a don\u0027t-care), it shouldn\u0027t^TM. Even better, because the NOT_READY state was very sparsely (and incorrectly) used, it\u0027s unlikely that legacy apps are incorrectly waiting for an explicit \"NOT_READY\" to do something.",
      "parentUuid": "4b99b92d_b737398b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 399,
        "startChar": 39,
        "endLine": 399,
        "endChar": 48
      },
      "revId": "71f529a1790feb70a08b92e99bcee52480bb6ca5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6ff9b4b8_0e189ee3",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/uicc/IccCardProxy.java",
